def make(i,prompt,opts,ans,exp,strat,steps,diff): return {"id":f"question-english-performance-2-{i}","subject":"english","type":"single-choice","prompt":prompt,"options":[{"id":k,"text":v} for k,v in opts.items()],"knowledgeIds":[KG],"difficulty":diff,"answer":{"value":ans,"explanation":exp},"provenance":{"origin":"original","license":"All rights reserved","sourceUrl":SOURCES[0]["url"],"sourceLocator":"三份公開英文試題僅供能力方向研究；未複製原題、選項、文章或答案。","authoringNote":"依官方課綱 KG 與公開英文試題的英文口語表達能力方向獨立編寫；待第二輪 AI／Terra 內容複核。"},"reviewStatus":"draft","updatedAt":"2026-09-09","lessonId":LESSON,"examPatternRefs":refs(),"solutionStrategy":strat,"solutionSteps":steps}
Q=[
make(1,"You meet a new exchange student. Which short introduction is most complete?",{"A":"Hi, I'm Amy from Class 8. I enjoy drawing, and I hope we can work together.","B":"Amy. Drawing. Finished.","C":"I am a classroom because exchange.","D":"You should know everything about me."},"A","A gives a greeting, name, class, interest, and friendly purpose, so it is a complete and natural introduction.","Check whether the spoken answer includes identity, one personal detail, and a connection to the listener.",["Identify the speaking purpose: introduce yourself.","Look for a name and class or background.","Look for one understandable personal interest.","Check for a friendly closing purpose and natural grammar.","Choose A because it organizes all needed information."],"easy"),
make(2,"Your partner asks what the school garden looks like. Which answer is a clear description?",{"A":"It is behind the library, with tall sunflowers and three wooden benches.","B":"I agree with the garden tomorrow.","C":"Please return the description to Monday.","D":"The garden can apologize."},"A","A identifies the location and gives visible details, which makes it a clear description.","Match the request for description with location and observable features rather than an opinion or unrelated action.",["Identify the object: the school garden.","Give a location: behind the library.","Add observable features: sunflowers and benches.","Reject agreement, return, and apology phrases.","Select A because the listener can picture the garden."],"easy"),
make(3,"You need your partner to speak more slowly during a practice talk. What should you say?",{"A":"Could you speak a little more slowly, please?","B":"You speak yesterday in a library.","C":"Stop the project and leave forever.","D":"I can compare your shoes."},"A","The sentence politely requests a slower speaking speed and directly addresses the communication need.","Identify the needed adjustment and use a polite could-you request with the relevant adverb.",["Notice the problem: the partner is speaking too fast.","Choose the action needed: speak more slowly.","Use Could you to make a polite request.","Reject unrelated time, leaving, and comparison statements.","Choose A because it is precise and cooperative."],"easy"),
make(4,"You choose a reusable bottle for a school project. Which spoken explanation gives a reason?",{"A":"I chose it because it reduces single-use plastic.","B":"I chose it because blue is a Tuesday.","C":"I choose it; no one can ask why.","D":"It is a bottle and therefore a pencil."},"A","Because it reduces single-use plastic supplies a logical reason for choosing a reusable bottle.","Look for a claim followed by a cause that actually relates to the chosen object.",["Identify the choice: a reusable bottle.","Find the connector because.","Check whether the following clause explains the choice.","Reject color-day, refusal, and contradictory statements.","Select A because the reason connects to reuse and waste reduction."],"medium"),
make(5,"You are giving a two-step safety report about a lab spill. Which order is clearest?",{"A":"First, keep away from the spill. Then, tell the teacher.","B":"Tell the teacher after everything is cleaned by someone else, first.","C":"The spill is a teacher, and safety is later.","D":"Keep away, maybe, but no one needs to know."},"A","First and Then clearly sequence the two safe actions: avoid the spill and notify the teacher.","Check for sequence markers and ensure the actions are safe, complete, and in a sensible order.",["Identify the first safety action: keep away.","Identify the reporting action: tell the teacher.","Look for clear order markers First and Then.","Reject confused, unsafe, or incomplete alternatives.","Choose A because the listener can follow the procedure."],"medium"),
make(6,"You must explain how two study methods differ. Which sentence makes a fair comparison?",{"A":"Flashcards help me review words quickly, while a study group lets me ask questions.","B":"Flashcards are good and the group is a window.","C":"Only my method deserves to exist.","D":"The study group happened before the alphabet."},"A","A describes one useful feature of each method and links them with while, making a balanced comparison.","Compare the same dimension of both options and avoid unsupported absolute judgments.",["Name both methods: flashcards and study group.","Give a relevant feature of flashcards.","Give a relevant feature of the group.","Use while to signal contrast without insulting either option.","Select A because it presents two evidence-based differences."],"medium"),
make(7,"A listener asks, 'Do you mean the north gate or the main gate?' You meant the north gate. What should you say?",{"A":"I mean the north gate, near the bicycle racks.","B":"Both gates are made of yesterday.","C":"Please forget every direction.","D":"I meant that the listener is a gate."},"A","A directly clarifies the intended gate and adds a landmark to prevent further confusion.","Answer the listener's either-or clarification first, then add a concrete location clue.",["Notice the ambiguity between two gates.","Select the intended one: the north gate.","Add a landmark: near the bicycle racks.","Reject abstract or insulting alternatives.","Choose A because it resolves the misunderstanding precisely."],"medium"),
make(8,"Your group cannot decide how to display its project. Which spoken suggestion is constructive?",{"A":"Why don't we ask two classmates to compare the layouts before choosing?","B":"Choose my layout or stop the project.","C":"The layout should apologize to the wall.","D":"No one can discuss a project."},"A","A proposes a specific, fair next step that can help the group compare options before deciding.","Choose a suggestion that advances decision-making and allows evidence or other viewpoints.",["Identify the group problem: it cannot choose a display.","Find the proposed action: ask classmates to compare.","Check that comparing layouts informs a later choice.","Reject threats, nonsense, and refusal to discuss.","Select A because it is practical and collaborative."],"hard"),
make(9,"You accidentally give your partner the wrong meeting time. Which spoken repair is best?",{"A":"I'm sorry—I gave you the wrong time. We should meet at 3:30 instead.","B":"The wrong time is your responsibility forever.","C":"I never spoke, so nothing needs changing.","D":"Congratulations on arriving at the wrong time."},"A","A acknowledges the error, apologizes, and supplies the corrected time and next action.","A complete repair should name the mistake, apologize, and provide usable corrected information.",["Identify the communication error: the time was wrong.","Use an apology to acknowledge responsibility.","Give the corrected time: 3:30.","Use instead to replace the earlier information.","Choose A because it repairs both the relationship and the plan."],"medium"),
make(10,"At the end of a presentation about saving water, which conclusion best summarizes the main message and action?",{"A":"Small daily choices, such as shorter showers, can save water, so our class will track its use this week.","B":"Water is a word, and the presentation has no point.","C":"We should use more water because the topic is water.","D":"The conclusion should introduce an unrelated sports game."},"A","A restates the idea that small choices matter and gives a related class action, making it an effective conclusion.","A conclusion should briefly restate the message and connect it to a realistic next step.",["Identify the topic: saving water.","Restate the main claim about small daily choices.","Give one relevant example: shorter showers.","Add the related action the class will take this week.","Choose A because it closes the presentation with message and action."],"hard"),
]
